n 5–10% pullbacks. That helps average cost and mitigates volatility risk.      • Set an initial stop-loss around 12–15% below your average entry to protect against unforeseen market shocks. Convert that into a trailing stop once you’re up 20%.      • Monitor catalysts quarterly: AI design wins, data-center revenue growth, margins and free-cash‐flow trends, and supply-chain developments. Reassess if AMD misses guidance or if a larger semiconductor downturn materializes.      • Plan to hold through near-term swings. Overlay a watch on technical levels: a decisive break below the 200-day SMA would signal a broader tech sell-off and trigger a partial exit or hedge.    By focusing on the bigger picture—AMD’s leadership in next-gen computing and robust financial footing—I’m comfortable with Buy. If you’ve been burned by chasing short-term signals in the past, this plan’s staggered entries and stop-loss guard against that mistake, while positioning you to capture the upside as AI and cloud workloads accelerate.
